00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.119 align:start position:0%
 
Rob Greene thank you so much for joining  me today my pleasure  Rob as a teacher what what kind of  things do you teach about in history  prior to this current situation what was  the course like in Quebec well I will  say that there was a many of the  problems that we we've been voicing our  concerns about with the new curriculum  many of those same problems existed in  the old curriculum so the last  curriculum was very much focused on the  the story of the Quebec nation as  opposed to the story of Quebec Society  however I will say particularly with the  the depictions of immigrant communities  to Quebec we saw a lot more in the old  curriculum than we're seeing in the new  curriculum now this curriculum was  developed by the parti québécois when  they were empowered briefly and we we  know that the parti québécois is a  separatist independent east they want to  Quebec to separate from Canada yeah who  prepared this course well the the PQ  launched this curriculum reform in the  normal way that curriculum reform is  done in Quebec and that began with a  committee of experts that had very  diverse representation however soon  after the the first couple meetings of  that more diverse committee happened  they essentially turned the curriculum  reform process over to a conservative  sovereigntist lobby group called la  coalition post-war a group that for the  last 20 years has been sort of banging  the drums in the media about the D  nationalization of Quebec history and  which is absurd because I just I just  told you like the old history curriculum  probably I didn't have them there's  haven't been a history curriculum in  Quebec that's had more references to the  Quebec nation so it's it is sort of an  absurd charge that they've been making  and literally the the curriculum reform  process was basically handed to them  these are very nationalists historians  who wrote it exactly and they're and  they're they're a group that's funded by  basically the most conservative elements  in the nationalist movement now what are  your concerns not as I understand it  when this was introduced  English teachers in Montreal and Quebec  were saying whoa this is beyond the pale  there's there are many many problems  with this history it is - it is very  nationalist it leaves out a lot of  actual history to promote a separatist  agenda yeah and and there was complaints  about that what happened well I should  actually say that initially teachers  were very supportive of this reform  because ostensibly it was being done for  reasons that had to do with the  structure of the program so it used to  be that year one of the program you'd  have the whole history of Quebec in  Canada and then year two you'd speed  through that whole history four times  focused on different themes and this was  very cumbersome for students so the  extensible reason for this reform was to  change the structure to make it more  like other history programs you know  half the history in year one and the  other half in year two however along the  way it's the cot it really became about  content as well and that's where both  teachers in the Anglophone sector but  also First Nations groups and and Inuit  groups began to speak up about what they  were seeing in that content and that was  basically you know the elimination of  Quebec semuc diversity we see no  reference to immigrant communities we  see the the recommendations of the Truth  and Reconciliation Commission had been  sort of ignored and Anglophones were  sort of presented as in the historical  narrative in the role of comic book  villains we're sort of presented as a  monolithic group of elites that are  there to sort of impede the progress of  Quebec at every turn and so it was all  of those issues that I think you know a  variety of different people started to  to raise awareness about now the  interesting thing of course is that the  separatist government introduced this  and there were complaints about it and  now we have since then a new government  supposedly a federalist leaning  government yeah who heard these  complaints I said we will correct them  and what has to be there well you know  that's the million dollar question at  you know we this has been  what has been so baffling about this and  you know theories range from just  straight-up government incompetence that  they didn't realize this reform process  that they inherited was underway and  just kind of let it continue until it  was sort of too late people are also  thinking maybe it's because they had  sent the textbooks to print before that  first wave of criticism last year and so  this is a money question yeah it could  be a money it could be a money question  but I know there's also the issue of how  sincere they were about making changes  so last week a press release was put out  by the Katta Vick school board basically  saying that the most recent round of  consultation of indigenous communities  that happened was a was a farce that and  and even worse than that  it was repeating colonial patterns of  the government dealing with indigenous  people that that really really need to  finally come to an end so in the history  that you are obliged to teach your  students if I'm understanding this  correctly leaves out almost entirely the  indigenous aspect of of Quebec and  Canada and and the Anglophone community  are treated sort of as you say villains  or disdainfully and impeding the  progress of yeah and there's there you  know in the first draft of the  curriculum there was not a single  reference to a positive contribution to  Quebec society by an Anglophone actor or  person in the in the revised draft there  is a single reference to a positive  contribution it does acknowledge that  Anglophones were active in the early  days of Quebec feminist movement but you  know to me the the really big omission  here is the Irish because the Irish were  Montreal's original working class they  were involved in some of the early labor  struggles they were a part of even you  know that that working-class history is  even part of the the early history of  the montreal canadiens that is a you  know very important in this city and in  this in this province so so to leave  that the Irish out of the picture  is really problematic America though I  mean as a history teacher when you talk  about Montreal which was at one time a  the single most important city in Canada  up until say the 1960s we had a city did  we I mean who built the city in that  sense as a modern city was it not the  Scottish and they may be ethically you  know McGill University and all the  hospitals no these are donated and so on  so forth I mean as as an Anglophone and  a historian yeah how do you feel about  this I mean well I you know I feel I  feel that that history is being hijacked  by a political agenda and and I want to  be clear that you know when I've been  criticizing the curriculum I'm not  saying that I want to replace one  ideologically driven narrative with  another I don't I'm not looking to  replace a sovereign as narrative with a  federalist narrative I'm looking for  students to be exposed to the range of  different perspectives that have existed  in Quebec society over the years because  it's only by exposing them to a range of  perspectives that they can develop  critical thinking skills and that's what  that's what a history course is is  supposed to be about so you know even  though you know I am an Anglophone  teaching in an English school board I  see one of my biggest roles as an  English an Anglophone teacher is to help  my Anglophone students understand the  perspectives of the francophone majority  and it's only that's how we can start to  build social cohesion and sort of break  down the notion of the two solitudes but  again if if you're teaching Anglophones  and want to teach them and expanded it  and I don't know if I should use work  more truthful he's very are what's  happening with the francophones who are  the majority are they being will shall I  use the word propagandized that's that  is that is the worry right like I'm I'm  actually not so concerned about that  like the history teachers that I know in  the English Montreal school board for  example they're they're excellent  teachers and they are going to be  supplementing the curriculum in a way  that does justice for their students but  as you as you point out the problem is  what about students up in lacks Asia or  what about even students meaning  a system whose teacher found out they  were teaching the program five days you  know before they started who doesn't  have the time to be preparing the the  supplemental material well I'm a  colleague of mine had a child in school  and that person said when the child went  to the history class the teacher said  here's the official thing the history  that you need for the test so learn that  yeah but here's the test of history that  I'm going to teach you which is very I  mean wow what message is that sending  then that and that's this is really part  of the problem for me as a teacher is  that there's all sorts of research out  there about how effective pedagogy  involves creating materials in which  students can see themselves reflected in  one way or another and when students  don't see and so when I think just my  school West not high it's a  multicultural school in downtown  Montreal this new curriculum is  rendering many of my students invisible  and sort of demonizing another group of  them so where do they see themselves  reflected they don't and that what that  does is that makes my job as a teacher  trying to get them to pass a  standardized test that much harder let  alone trying to get them to develop a  love of history okay you know which  which ultimately should is should be my  most important priority but because of  these ministry exams I sometimes have to  focus on getting them through that is  there a problem in Canada in the fact  that we don't have a countrywide history  agenda but it's controlled by the  provinces well I mean there is there's a  lot of justification to that just for  example you know I think you know  Canada's history is vast and I think it  is it does make sense for example that  that British Columbia's history  curriculum would be focused on some  different things but I well I see it  maybe in another way that I think it's  problematic in any public education  system in a democratic society that that  people that are part of that society are  being eliminated from the  Oracle narrative I just think is the  role of public education should be about  helping us understand each other  building social cohesion helping  understand helping students better  understand and think critically about  the society in which they live what's  the solution Rob Green I mean your group  is asking for changes yeah what's what's  going to happen what do you think what  happened we've we've launched an online  petition and we've gotten a lot of  signatures already the and we like thus  far the the efforts that we've made in  speaking out I think they have had an  impact like there have been some changes  that have been made but I think it's  going to take more so you know whether  this so basically what we've demanded of  government in our petition is sort of  two things in the short term to  immediately work with school boards and  other stakeholders to develop  supplemental materials to immediately  supplement what's there because we  realize the government isn't going to  overnight produce new textbooks and have  a whole new program so we've got a short  term goal that's sort of the you know a  band-aid solution but then the long term  solution is extensive public  consultations of all the various  communities to make sure that this is  being done right because again when you  know reading about the the consultations  that weren't consultations of indigenous  communities this is this is awful this  is the opposite direction that we should  be going and particularly in light of  the recommendations of the Truth and  Reconciliation Commission I mean one of  one of those recommendations is that  every provincial Minister of Education  should have an expert in indigenous  history as sort of an assistant to them  and to my knowledge that does not happen  in Quebec and it needs to happen and had  it happened we would not be seeing some  of the problems in the curriculum that  we see now Rob green thank you very much  for coming in today  great pleasure

